{
  "id": "Compound::DB00761 - Gene::9990",
  "verbalization": "Potassium Chloride (KCl) is shown to interact with multiple members of the Slc12 family of chloride transporters, specifically Slc12a5, Slc12a7, and Slc12a2, through binding interactions. These transporters are involved in the regulation of cation-coupled chloride transport, a process critical for maintaining cellular chloride homeostasis. The shared involvement of these transporters in the same functional pathway suggests a coordinated role in chloride transport across cellular membranes. The presence of a common downstream target, Slc12a6, indicates that these transporters may collectively contribute to the overall function of chloride transport in the cell. The evidence from the three paths, while not directly linking KCl to Slc12a6, collectively supports a mechanism where KCl influences the activity of these transporters, which in turn affect Slc12a6. The observed scores suggest a plausible and consistent relationship, though further experimental validation would be necessary to confirm the direct interaction and functional significance of KCl in modulating Slc12a6 activity.",
